>> explain how we plan to better resolve this in the future.
>>
>> The immediate fix can be seen below with small (perhaps unsettling)
>> revisions to your example. This code runs as you wished in R 3.0.0:
>>
>> library(Zelig)
>> library(ZeligChoice)
>>
>> data(mexico)
>>
>> dem.func<-function(){
>> z.out<-zelig(as.factor(vote88)**~pristr + othcok + othsocok,
>> model="mlogit", data=mexico)
>> x.weak<-setx(z.out, pristr=aaa, othcok=bbb, othsocok=ccc)
>> return(x.weak)
>> }
>>
>> aaa<-1
>> bbb<-2
>> ccc<-3
>> output<-dem.func()
>>
>> print(summary(output))
>>
>> The change here is that the arguments you wanted to pass are defined in
>> the global environment outside the function. Within the function, the
>> variables passed to the zelig arguments are now "free variables" defined by
>> the (lexical) scoping of R. That is generally undesirable coding practice,
>> but notice, even in your original example, the dataset "mexico" was outside
>> the scope of your function; the data wasn't passed as an argument. (If it
>> had been, you would have seen an error in the zelig() call before getting
>> to the setx() call, which is what I meant by this being a more general
>> artifact.)
>>
>> That approach gives you one solution to your immediate problem.
>>
>> This issue comes about, because as Zelig4 was being developed, the
>> original designers were trying to make an object oriented structure,
>> working within the limitations (and problems) of the S4 class. Currently,
>> we plan to shift things this summer to utilize the newer R5 class and its
>> improvements, and remove Zelig's reliance on the environment.
