Ari,
This is now fixed on the current available Beta build.  Thanks for pointing this out.  The example code below runs (including install from the Beta repository).  Note the change to the Data Generation Process which creates fewer numerical issues for the estimator:


install.packages("Zelig", repos="http://r.iq.harvard.edu", type="source")

library(Zelig)


data(turnout)

dat <- turnout


dat$count <- rnbinom(nrow(dat),mu=exp(0.01*dat$age),size=3)


Zrobust <- zelig(count ~ age, model = "negbinom", data = dat)

print(summary(Zrobust))

zelnb_setxLow <- setx( Zrobust, age=10 )

zelnb_setxHigh <- setx( Zrobust, age=50 )


zelnb_simIncChange <- sim(Zunrobust, x = zelnb_setxLow, x1 = zelnb_setxHigh)


print(summary(zelnb_simIncChange))


plot(zelnb_simIncChange)


I'm not happy with how the matrix plot comparing predicted values at different x's looks when there is a count variable with many values (bottom left of the plot).  I'll likely revise that for these count models in the coming week.  Ideas are certainly welcome.

James

