>>> FYI, Previously, after the warning message, you were getting the
>>> negative binomial results without Robust standard errors.  That is how they
>>> come from glm.nb function available in R.  Now that the hook is fixed the
>>> model will be estimated with robust standard errors.  Currently the
>>> "robust=FALSE" argument, that you put in your example, will not turn them
>>> off, although this could change in the next couple weeks.  If you want to
>>> explore turning off Robust standard errors in the meantime, let me know and
>>> I can forward code to overwrite the definition of the negative binomial
>>> model in Zelig.
>>>
>>> Tangentially, there is the more general question of whether robust
>>> standard errors are appropriate given that the negative binomial model
>>> already models overdispersion.  The combination of robust standard errors
>>> with Poisson coefficient estimates (often called the Poisson quasi maximium
>>> likelihood estimator) is very common in Poisson modelling for example,
>>> because Poisson does not account for overdispersion.  It might seem then,
>>> that if negative binomial models explicitly model overdispersion, then
>>> robust standard errors are superfluous.  However, the various negative
>>> binomial models that exist (NB1, NB2, etc.) each have very specific
>>> functional forms for how the variance moves as a function of the mean.  If
>>> in the data, that particular functional form is wrong, then the standard
>>> errors are wrong, although the NB2 form is flexible enough that it
>>> reasonably approximates a range of misspecified cases.  Pragmatically
>>>  Wooldridge (2002, section 19.3.2 p.659) and Cameron and Trivedi (2009,
>>> section 17.3.3 p.563) both suggest robust standard errors for the NB2
>>> negative binomial estimator which Zelig uses, noting that it generally
>>> produces only small differences.  Offering the option to remove the robust
>>> standard errors is an expansion we are now exploring.
>>>
>>> James
